<Project>
  <!--
    The LATE half of the package's build contribution. The CoreCLR pin itself is in the .props
    beside this file, because a default has to be set before the Android SDK sets its own; see the
    import-order note there (#172).

    What belongs here is the opposite kind of thing: checks and explanations that need the FINAL
    answer. By the time this file is imported the workload has resolved _AndroidRuntime, so both
    targets below can see what the app is actually going to ship.
  -->

  <!--
    Say why XA1040 is there.

    Choosing CoreCLR makes the Android SDK emit XA1040: "The CoreCLR runtime on Android is an
    experimental feature and not yet suitable for production use." The warning is accurate and it is
    Microsoft's to make — but it is silent about the fact that the choice was FORCED, and a consumer
    reading it has nothing at all connecting it to this package. That is a warning about production
    readiness, arriving in a build the developer did not configure, with no visible cause.

    So the cause is printed next to it. The full shape of the trade is worth stating plainly:

      * Mono is the ONLY runtime XA1040 does not warn about (its condition is "not MonoVM"; NativeAOT
        trips it too) — and Mono is the one that does not work.
      * So there is no configuration here that is both warning-free and functional, and the warning
        cannot be "fixed" at this end. It clears when CoreCLR on Android stops being experimental,
        which is a different upstream event from Mono's defect being repaired.

    A MESSAGE, not a warning. A package that adds a warning to every consumer's build for something
    the consumer cannot act on is noise, and it would break anyone building with
    TreatWarningsAsErrors — turning an explanatory note into a build failure is precisely the
    opposite of the point. High importance, so it survives normal verbosity and CI logs.
  -->
  <Target Name="CupriFaceExplainCoreClrRuntime" BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(CupriFacePinnedCoreClr)' == 'true'
                     and '$(_AndroidRuntime)' != 'MonoVM'
                     and '$(EnablePreviewFeatures)' != 'true'
                     and '$(CupriFaceQuietRuntimeNote)' != 'true'">
    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="CupriFace.Android: the XA1040 warning about an experimental runtime is expected here, and this package is the reason for it. CupriFace pins UseMonoRuntime=false because the Mono runtime pack in .NET 10 miscompiles the engine — a code-generation defect in Mono's string comparison path that crashes the app during startup, on device, before the first frame (forensics: samples/AndroidProbe/MONO-CRASH.md in the CupriFace repository). Mono is the only runtime XA1040 does not warn about and the only one that does not work, so there is no setting here that is both quiet and functional. Set CupriFaceQuietRuntimeNote=true to silence this note; it does not silence XA1040." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    And refuse the configuration that crashes.

    Why it is worth an error rather than a warning, where the note above is not: a consumer who ends
    up on Mono has no way to find out except by shipping an APK that dies in OnCreate, on a device,
    with a managed exception whose stack points at innocent string code. That is the single most
    expensive failure this package can hand someone, and it is completely invisible at build time.
    Turning it into a build error that names the cause costs a consumer who genuinely wants Mono one
    property.
  -->
  <Target Name="CupriFaceCheckAndroidRuntime" BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(UseMonoRuntime)' == 'true' and '$(CupriFaceAllowMonoRuntime)' != 'true'">
    <Error Code="CUPRI0001"
           Text="CupriFace.Android requires the CoreCLR runtime, and this app is building against Mono (UseMonoRuntime=true, _AndroidRuntime=$(_AndroidRuntime)). This is not a preference: the Mono runtime pack in .NET 10 (10.0.11) has a code-generation defect that miscompiles the engine's string handling, and a CupriFace app built against it crashes during startup, in OnCreate, before the first frame. It reproduces on arm64 hardware and x64 emulators, under AOT and JIT alike, trimmed or not. CoreCLR runs the identical IL correctly and is the supported configuration — it is what this package defaults you to, and the XA1040 'experimental runtime' warning that comes with it is the known cost of the workaround. Remove UseMonoRuntime from this project to take that default, or set CupriFaceAllowMonoRuntime=true to build against Mono anyway and accept the startup crash. Forensics: samples/AndroidProbe/MONO-CRASH.md." />
  </Target>
</Project>
